+
+## Lemma L1: sharp conditional bound for disjoint edge sets
+
+Let G=(V,E) be finite, with an arbitrary boundary partition xi. Let phi=phi^xi_{G,p,q}, where 0<p<1 and q>=1. For pairwise disjoint, nonempty edge sets C_1,...,C_n put A_i={at least one edge in C_i is open}. Then
+
+    phi(intersection_{i=1}^n A_i) <= product_{i=1}^n [1-(1-p)^{|C_i|}].
+
+This holds whether the C_i are geometric cutsets or not. If every ordinary open path from x to a target set must meet every C_i, the same upper bound holds for that path event. It is sharp as a bound on the intersection of the A_i: for q=1 the A_i are independent and equality holds. No independence is asserted for q>1.
+
+Proof. Removing one edge e, opening it reduces the number of xi-components by either zero or one. The full conditional open probability is respectively p or p/[p+q(1-p)], so the conditional closed probability is at least 1-p. The same bound holds after conditioning on any positive-probability event measurable without e, by averaging the full conditional probability. For an event D measurable outside C, reveal the edges of C in order, each time conditioning also on all previously revealed edges being closed. The chain rule gives phi(all C closed | D)>=(1-p)^{|C|}. Take D=intersection_{j<i} A_j: disjointness makes D measurable outside C_i. Thus phi(A_i | D)<=1-(1-p)^{|C_i|}; the probability chain rule proves the product. If a prefix event has probability zero, the result is immediate. At q=1 all edges are independent Bernoulli(p), giving the equality claim. QED.
+
+On a locally finite graph, the inequality passes to any free or wired infinite-volume limit for the finite cylinder events A_i. Consequently, if disjoint finite cutsets C_i separate a fixed vertex from infinity and sum_i (1-p)^{|C_i|}=infinity, that vertex has no infinite ordinary open cluster. Indeed its infinite-cluster event requires every A_i, and the n-cut bound is at most exp(-sum_{i<=n}(1-p)^{|C_i|}), which tends to zero. In particular, |C_i|<=c log(i+1)+K implies absence when c |log(1-p)|<=1, including equality. Existence of such cutsets is an indispensable hypothesis.
+
+## Lemma L2: finite-width strips have no finite-temperature order
+
+Let H be any finite connected simple graph with m vertices and let G=Z Cartesian-product H. For every q>=1 and 0<p<1, set rho=1-(1-p)^m<1. For any FK boundary partition in a finite interval of layers, the probability of an ordinary open path from layer 0 to layer n is at most rho^n, provided that all n intervening cuts are contained in the domain. Each cut consists of the m longitudinal edges between two neighboring layers. A path may wander in either direction, but it must use at least one edge of every intervening cut. L1 gives the estimate.
+
+An ordinary open path from x in layer 0 to either end layer of [-n,n] Cartesian-product H has probability at most 2 rho^n. An infinite cluster must reach one of those end layers for every n, since each finite interval contains finitely many vertices. Hence all ordinary open clusters are finite for 0<p<1 under both free and wired infinite-volume FK limits. The case p=0 is immediate because every edge is closed. At p=1 the graph is connected, so the ordinary percolation threshold is exactly 1.
+
+For integer q>=2, give both end layers color 1. In the Edwards-Sokal coupling, the boundary cluster is color 1 and each other cluster gets a uniform color. Therefore normalized magnetization at an interior vertex is exactly the probability of an ordinary open path to an end layer, bounded by 2 rho^n. Taking n to infinity gives m(beta)=0 for every finite beta, since p=1-exp(-beta)<1. Thus beta_c=infinity for this strip. This conclusion is about spontaneous magnetization; no claim of latent heat or free-energy analyticity is needed. QED.
+
+## Corollary L3: precise obstruction to a degree/amenability bridge
+
+Choose H=K_5. G=Z Cartesian-product K_5 is simple, connected, vertex transitive, and six-regular: there are four neighbors in a fiber and two longitudinal neighbors. The sets F_n=[-n,n] Cartesian-product K_5 have |F_n|=5(2n+1) and ten edges leaving them, hence their boundary-to-volume ratio tends to zero and G is amenable. By L2 the q=3 Potts model has no spontaneous magnetization at any finite beta, despite coordination number six.
+
+This refutes the stronger hypothetical bridge that q>=3, six-regularity, transitivity and amenability alone imply finite-temperature order or a finite-temperature first-order transition. It does not refute C0048: this graph has linear growth and is not Z^3. It also does not refute a bridge that additionally assumes p_c<1, which fails here. The exact failed implication and these scope exclusions belong with the result whenever recalled.
+
+## Single hardest remaining gap
+
+Establish theta^w(p_c,3)>0 on the actual nearest-neighbor lattice Z^3. A valid route needs a lattice-specific ordered-state or interface estimate at the critical parameter with constants strong enough for q=3. The finite-energy calculation above gives upper bounds on connectivity across bottlenecks; it supplies no positive critical lower bound. On Z^3, natural finite nested separators grow quadratically, and the divergent-cutset condition above does not follow. All large-q, large-d, spread-out and tree inputs retain their own hypotheses.
+
+## Exact regression witness and scope correction
+
+Running `python -X utf8 ~/brain_workers/w462/check_cutsets.py` checked 3,456 weighted configurations of the width-two, three-layer ladder, for q in {1,2,3}, p in {1/3,1/2,2/3}, and three boundary partitions. All 12,096 single-edge conditionals, 1,728 full-cut conditionals, 81 partial-history conditionals and 27 product upper bounds passed. All nine q=1 cases attained equality. This finite computation is a regression check of the elementary proof, not a formal proof of the general result.
+
+With free boundary, q=3,p=1/2, let A and B say that the respective two-edge longitudinal cuts contain an open edge. Exact probabilities are P(A)=P(B)=409/925 and P(A intersection B)=181/925. Thus the joint probability exceeds the product of marginals by 144/855625>0. The cuts are disjoint genuine separators of the ladder. An attempted upper bound formed by multiplying these marginals fails. In the full 27-case suite, that invalid marginal-product bound failed 18 cases; the correctly conditioned L1 bound passed every case. The ledger's witness probability is about cut events, not actual point-to-point connectivity (which is 88/925 in this case).
+
+These fractions also have a short exact derivation independent of floating-point arithmetic. At p=1/2 the common factor cancels and each configuration has weight q^k. The ladder partition polynomial is q^6+7q^5+21q^4+37q^3+39q^2+23q, giving Z(3)=5550. Closing either longitudinal cut leaves an isolated two-vertex edge and a four-cycle, with partition weights (q^2+q) and (q^4+4q^3+6q^2+5q), whose product at q=3 is 12 times 258=3096. Closing both cuts leaves three isolated edges, with weight 12^3=1728. Inclusion-exclusion gives weight(A)=5550-3096=2454 and weight(A intersection B)=5550-2(3096)+1728=1086, proving the stated probabilities. The polynomial counts need independent checking along with the general proof.
+
+The C0048 intake row adds a parenthetical identification with q_c(d)=2. If q_c is a threshold for real cluster weights, this is stronger than the integer-Potts target and is not a logical equivalence. The monotone predicate D(q) defined by q>5/2 has D(2)=false and D(n)=true for every integer n>=3, yet its real threshold is 5/2. This is a countermodel to an implication between quantifier statements, not a construction of a random-cluster phase diagram. If q_c is defined only from integer states, the notation requires a separate definition. The source's genuine lattice conjecture is not refuted. Record this as a conditional scope warning until the referee checks the cited ICM context.
